---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";

const deps = [
  { name: "eframe / egui", note: "Cross-platform immediate-mode GUI" },
  { name: "reqwest", note: "HTTP client" },
  { name: "sequoia-openpgp", note: "PGP verification" },
  { name: "sha2", note: "SHA-256 hashing" },
  { name: "directories", note: "Platform paths" },
  { name: "serde / serde_json", note: "JSON parsing" },
];
---

<Layout title="About" description="About the developer behind Tor Browser Installer.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
      <h1>About me</h1>
      
      <p class="lede">
        I'm Ribhav. I built Tor Browser Installer because the installation process 
        was unnecessarily manual. You download a browser to download a browser, then 
        you're left with a folder to extract or an app to drag. So I made a tool that 
        handles the entire process: download, verify, install, launch.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:40px;">Why this project exists</h2>
      <p>
        Tor Browser doesn't ship with a proper installer. You get a compressed archive 
        or a drag-and-drop app. That's fine for power users, but most people expect an 
        installer that handles verification and placement automatically. This project 
        fills that gap without introducing new trust assumptions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every download is verified with SHA-256 and PGP signatures from the Tor Browser 
        Developers key before anything touches your system. If verification fails, 
        installation stops. No exceptions.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:40px;">Other work</h2>
      <p>
        I have another GitHub account: <a href="https://github.com/N00B-5634">N00B-5634</a>.
        There you'll find my NixOS configurations and an OpenIndiana project. The NixOS 
        configs are my daily driver, and the OpenIndiana work is an exploration of 
        illumos-based systems.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:40px;">FAQ</h2>
      
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Why not just use the official Tor Browser?</h3>
          <p>
            The official download is just the browser bundle. There's no installer that 
            verifies the download, places it correctly, and sets up launching. This tool 
            does all of that automatically.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Why Rust and egui?</h3>
          <p>
            Rust gives memory safety and performance without a runtime. egui provides 
            a simple immediate-mode GUI that compiles to native code. No Electron, no 
            web view, no bloat. Small, fast binary that works the same on Windows, 
            macOS, and Linux.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Is this affiliated with the Tor Project?</h3>
          <p>No. Independent project. I built it because I wanted a better installation experience.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What's the current status?</h3>
          <p>
            Version 0.2.0, beta. Core verification is solid, but some interfaces and 
            defaults are still being refined. Check the <a href="/security">security page</a> 
            for details.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:40px;">License</h2>
      <p>
        AGPL-3.0. Full text in the repository's LICENSE file.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:40px;">Built with</h2>
      <ul class="deps">
        {deps.map((d) => (
          <li><span class="mono">{d.name}</span><span>{d.note}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:40px;">Acknowledgments</h2>
      <ul class="ack">
        <li><a href="https://www.torproject.org/">The Tor Project</a> &mdash; for Tor Browser.</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/emilk/egui">egui</a> &mdash; for the GUI framework.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.rust-lang.org/">Rust</a> &mdash; for the language.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:40px;">Contributing</h2>
      <p>
        Open an issue or pull request on GitHub. Run <code>cargo check</code> and 
        <code>cargo clippy</code> before submitting.
      </p>

      <p style="margin-top:32px;" class="disclaimer">
        This is an unofficial, third-party tool. Not affiliated with or endorsed by 
        The Tor Project. "Tor" and the onion logo are trademarks of The Tor Project, Inc.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .narrow { max-width: 720px; }
  .faq {
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
  }
  .faq-item {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--app-border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }
  .faq-item h3 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--app-purple-deep);
    margin: 0 0 12px 0;
  }
  .faq-item p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .deps { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 10px; }
  .deps li {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--app-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .deps li span:last-child { color: var(--text-muted); text-align: right; }
  .ack { padding-left: 22px; display: grid; gap: 8px; }
  .ack li { color: var(--text); }
  .ack a { color: var(--tor-purple); }
  .disclaimer { 
    font-size: 0.85rem; 
    color: var(--app-text-secondary); 
    border-top: 1px solid var(--app-border); 
    padding-top: 20px; 
  }
</style>
